From 113dc4522ac441ae99eb38ec4eee422538255bcf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cyril Hrubis <metan@ucw.cz>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 17:03:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] select: calc() fix crash (SIGFPE) by division by zero

The parser does not refuse a zero divisor, and an expression with an
operand it cannot fold e.g. `calc(150vw / 0)`, which reaches the
selection-time calculator as bytecode, where css_divide_fixed() divided
by 0 and the process dies on SIGFPE. This bug was found by fuzzing.

CSS Values 4, 10.9: x / 0 is an infinity of x's sign, clamped to the
largest value the property takes, and 0 / 0 is NaN, which a top-level
calculation turns into 0. The calculator now returns signed infinity
instead of crashing libcss.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Cyril Hrubis <metan@ucw.cz>
---
 src/select/calc.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/select/calc.c b/src/select/calc.c
index 387188e..e19c56b 100644
--- a/src/select/calc.c
+++ b/src/select/calc.c
@@ -262,7 +262,14 @@ css_error css_calculator_calculate(css_calculator *calc,
 			if (op == CALC_MULTIPLY) {
 				v_left = css_multiply_fixed(v_left, v_right);
 			} else {
-				v_left = css_divide_fixed(v_left, v_right);
+				if (v_right == 0) {
+					if (v_left > 0)
+						v_left = INT32_MAX;
+					else if (v_left < 0)
+						v_left = INT32_MIN;
+				} else {
+					v_left = css_divide_fixed(v_left, v_right);
+				}
 			}
 
 			CALC_PUSH(u_left, v_left);
